litecoin-seeder
===============

Litecoin-seeder is a crawler for the Litecoin network, which exposes a list
of reliable nodes via a built-in DNS server.

Features:
* regularly revisits known nodes to check their availability
* bans nodes after enough failures, or bad behaviour
* accepts nodes down to v0.5.0 to request new IP addresses from,
  but only reports good post-v0.6.9 nodes.
* keeps statistics over (exponential) windows of 2 hours, 8 hours,
  1 day and 1 week, to base decisions on.
* very low memory (a few tens of megabytes) and cpu requirements.
* crawlers run in parallel (by default 96 threads simultaneously).

REQUIREMENTS
------------

$ sudo apt-get install build-essential libboost-all-dev libssl-dev

USAGE
-----

Assuming you want to run a dns seed on dnsseed.example.com, you will
need an authorative NS record in example.com's domain record, pointing
to for example vps.example.com:

$ dig -t NS dnsseed.example.com

;; ANSWER SECTION
dnsseed.example.com.   86400    IN      NS     vps.example.com.

On the system vps.example.com, you can now run dnsseed:

./dnsseed -h dnsseed.example.com -n vps.example.com

If you want the DNS server to report SOA records, please provide an
e-mail address (with the @ part replaced by .) using -m.

COMPILING
---------

Compiling will require boost and ssl.  On debian systems, these are provided
by `libboost-dev` and `libssl-dev` respectively.

$ make

This will produce the `dnsseed` binary.

BLACKLISTING
------------

A DNS seed publishes A/AAAA records for machines it does not control, so its
domain accumulates whatever reputation those addresses carry. `dnsseed` can
suppress nodes from DNS answers using a local blacklist file, DNSBL lookups, or
both.

The DNSBL check fails closed. An address is published only when every required
zone has affirmatively said it is not listed, and said so recently. An address
that has never been checked, or whose verdict has expired, is not published.
This is the difference between suppressing bad addresses after something
notices them and not publishing addresses that have not been cleared.

Checks run in a background thread, never while answering a DNS request.

Blacklist file
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

One IP address or CIDR range per line. `#` starts a comment.

```
# Exact IPs
203.0.113.10

# IPv4 and IPv6 ranges
198.51.100.0/24
2001:4860:4860::/48
```

```
./dnsseed -h dnsseed.example.com -n vps.example.com -m hostmaster.example.com \
  --blacklist blacklist.txt
```

The file is reloaded every `--blacklist-refresh` seconds, so a cron job that
rewrites it (from Spamhaus DROP, Team Cymru bogons, or your own additions) takes
effect without a restart. File entries are also the only filter applied to the
crawler itself: a listed address is never added to the database at all.

DNSBL zones
~~~~~~~~~~~

```
--dnsbl zone[:codes][:v6][:canary=<ip>][:canary6=[<ip>]]
```

`codes` is a comma separated list of final octets, or `lo-hi` ranges, that count
as a listing. **Set this.** With no codes, any answer in `127.0.0.0/8` counts,
and for `zen` that includes `127.0.0.10` and `127.0.0.11`, which are PBL — they
mean only that the address is a dynamic or residential IP that should not be
sending mail directly. That describes a large share of honest nodes and says
nothing about reputation. The codes worth acting on in `zen` are:

```
  2      SBL
  3      SBL CSS
  4-7    XBL (CBL)
  9      DROP
  10,11  PBL -- do NOT include
```

```
./dnsseed -h dnsseed.example.com -n vps.example.com -m hostmaster.example.com \
  --dnsbl 'zen.dq.spamhaus.net:2,3,4-7,9'
```

Addresses are queried with the octets reversed, `203.0.113.10` as
`10.113.0.203.<zone>`. A zone marked `:v6` is also queried for IPv6 nodes, with
the nibbles reversed the way `ip6.arpa` does. Without `:v6` a zone is not
consulted about IPv6 at all, and IPv6 nodes are then filtered only by
`--blacklist`; `dnsseed` warns about this at startup.

A zone passed with `--dnsbl-advisory` instead of `--dnsbl` still suppresses what
it lists, but is not required to answer for an address to be cleared. Use it for
a second opinion you do not want to be a dependency.

Canaries
~~~~~~~~

Every zone is asked about an address it is known to list, by default
`127.0.0.2`, the conventional DNSBL test entry. If that lookup does not come
back listed, the zone is not answering us and is dropped from the pass: it
clears nothing, and a `canary-fail` line goes to the blacklist log and the
console status line.

This is not paranoia. An expired or mistyped Spamhaus DQS key answers NXDOMAIN,
which is indistinguishable from "not listed" — without the canary, a dead key
silently clears every address it is asked about and filtering quietly stops.

Verdicts already recorded are left alone when a canary fails, so a broken key
degrades over `--blacklist-max-age` rather than emptying the seed instantly.
That window is the time you have to notice and fix it.

An IPv6 canary must be bracketed, because its colons would otherwise be read as
spec separators: `canary6=[2001:db8::1]`. Set one on any `:v6` zone — a zone
that answers IPv4 but is blind to IPv6 otherwise looks like it is clearing every
IPv6 node. `canary=off` disables the check, and is a bad idea.

Resolver
~~~~~~~~

Use a local recursive resolver or Spamhaus DQS. Avoid large public resolvers
such as `1.1.1.1` or `8.8.8.8` for DNSBL checks; they can produce blocked,
rewritten, or false-negative answers, and Spamhaus returns `127.255.255.254`
for its free zones when queries arrive through one. Answers in
`127.255.255.0/24` are treated as query or configuration errors, never as
listings and never as clearances.

```
--dnsbl-resolver 127.0.0.1:53
```

A DQS key is part of the zone name, so keep it out of the repository and out of
shell history — pass it from a systemd unit or an environment file with
restricted permissions. `dnsseed` redacts it to `KEY.zen.dq.spamhaus.net` in
every log line and in the verdict cache, so the key never reaches disk through
them.

Timing and persistence
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

```
--blacklist-refresh 3600    # recheck a verdict after this long
--blacklist-max-age 21600   # a verdict stops counting after this long
--blacklist-cache <file>    # keep verdicts across restarts
```

A pass runs every minute and checks up to 250 addresses that have no verdict or
a verdict due for renewal, never checked first, then least recently checked. A
newly good node is therefore usually publishable within a minute rather than
waiting a full refresh interval.

The max age is held to at least three times the refresh interval, so one
resolver outage cannot expire every verdict at once.

Query volume is roughly (good nodes) x (required zones) / (refresh interval), so
2000 good nodes against one zone at the default 3600 is about 48k lookups a day.
Check that against your DQS plan, and raise `--blacklist-refresh` if it does not
fit; the pass cap of 250 addresses per minute is well above what that needs.

Without `--blacklist-cache`, a restart starts from no verdicts and publishes
nothing until the first passes complete. With it, verdicts survive.

Rolling it out
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Strict clearance can remove a lot of nodes if a zone or its codes are
misconfigured. Measure first:

```
--blacklist-report-only
```

Unchecked and stale addresses are then counted but still published, while file
matches and real listings are still suppressed. The console status line and the
`tally` line in the blacklist log show what strict mode would cost:

```
2026-05-31T05:17:08Z tally total=321 publish=290 file=2 listed=7 unchecked=20 stale=2
```

When `unchecked` and `stale` settle near zero and `listed` looks plausible, drop
the flag.

Logs
~~~~

```
--blacklist-log <file>
```

Refresh progress, canary failures, and listing transitions. Peers already listed
are not rewritten on every pass.

```
2026-05-31T05:16:55Z refresh-start peers=321 due=45 outstanding=45 zones=2 file_entries=0
2026-05-31T05:16:58Z refresh-progress processed=50/45 checked=50 errors=0 listed=1
2026-05-31T05:17:00Z listed 203.0.113.10 KEY.zen.dq.spamhaus.net=127.0.0.2
2026-05-31T05:17:08Z refresh-finish processed=45/45 checked=45 errors=0 listed=7 file_entries=0 dnsbl_listed=7
2026-05-31T05:17:08Z tally total=321 publish=290 file=2 listed=7 unchecked=20 stale=2
2026-05-31T06:17:00Z unlisted 203.0.113.10
2026-05-31T07:17:00Z canary-fail KEY.zen.dq.spamhaus.net did not list its canary 127.0.0.2
```

```
--served-log <file>
```

What the seed actually published, and when. This is the record to reach for when
an abuse desk names an address and a date: without it you cannot confirm the
address, deny it, or show that you stopped.

It logs the eligible set rather than individual answers. A DNS answer is a
random sample of that set, so the eligible set is a superset of everything the
domain could have resolved to, and it does not record who asked.

```
2026-05-31T05:16:55Z serve-snapshot count=290
2026-05-31T05:16:55Z serve-set 198.51.100.7
2026-05-31T05:17:55Z serve-add 198.51.100.9
2026-05-31T05:18:55Z serve-drop 198.51.100.7
```

A full snapshot is written at startup and hourly; transitions are written in
between. On a seed with a couple of thousand nodes that is a few MB a day, so
point logrotate at it — but keep the history long enough to outlive the
complaints you may need to answer with it. `dnsseed` appends and never truncates,
so `copytruncate` or a post-rotate restart both work.

ANSWER TTL
----------

```
--ttl <seconds>    # default 3600
```

The TTL on A/AAAA answers trades off two opposing risks, and the default is
deliberately the higher one.

Lowering it bounds how long a removed address keeps being handed out by
resolvers that already cached it. That matters only in proportion to how often
you actually need to remove an address.

Raising it weakens a fast-flux signature. A domain that answers with a dozen
addresses, drawn from a large pool spread across many networks, rotating on a
short TTL, queried by thousands of hosts that keep re-querying, looks like
botnet command-and-control to automated classifiers -- because that is exactly
what fast-flux C&C looks like. A DNS seed matches the pattern honestly, and
domains have been listed on Spamhaus DBL as `127.0.1.6` (botnet C&C) for it.

Unless you are routinely pulling listed addresses out of the seed, the second
risk is the larger one, so prefer a long TTL. See "Reducing the flux signature"
below.

REDUCING THE FLUX SIGNATURE
---------------------------

Blacklisting individual nodes and avoiding a fast-flux classification are
different problems, and the second one is not solved by the first. A seed whose
every node is spotless can still be listed for the shape of its DNS answers.

What drives the classification, in rough order of weight:

* how fast the published set rotates
* how many distinct addresses an observer can collect by re-querying
* the TTL
* how widely the addresses are spread across networks
* how much of the pool is in dynamic or residential space

The first is the one with the most headroom. `GetIPs_` draws a fresh random
sample of up to half the good set on every answer-cache rebuild, and the cache
rebuilds within seconds under load, so an observer polling the seed sees the
entire pool cycle through a few addresses at a time. That is the single
strongest flux signal the seeder emits, and it buys nothing: clients need a
handful of reachable peers, not a different handful every few seconds.

The mitigations, cheapest first:

* keep `--ttl` high (see above)
* keep the published set stable over time rather than resampling constantly
* serve the seed from a domain that carries nothing else, so a listing cannot
  reach your mail or web reputation

The last one is not a mitigation of the pattern, it is containment of the blast
radius, and it is the only one that is certain to work. A DBL listing lands on
the registered domain and everything under it, so a seed on a subdomain of a
company domain puts that company's mail on the same listing.

TESTING
-------

It's sometimes useful to test `dnsseed` locally to ensure it's giving good
output (either as part of development or sanity checking). You can inspect
`dnsseed.dump` to inspect all nodes being tracked for crawling, or you can
issue DNS requests directly. Example:

$ dig @:: -p 15353 dnsseed.example.com
       ^       ^    ^
       |       |    |__ Should match the host (-h) argument supplied to dnsseed
       |       |
       |       |_______ Port number (example uses the user space port; see below)
       |
       |_______________ Explicitly call the DNS server on localhost


RUNNING AS NON-ROOT
-------------------

Typically, you'll need root privileges to listen to port 53 (name service).

One solution is using an iptables rule (Linux only) to redirect it to
a non-privileged port:

$ iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -p udp --dport 53 -j REDIRECT --to-port 15353

If properly configured, this will allow you to run dnsseed in userspace, using
the -p 15353 option.

Another solution is allowing a binary to bind to ports < 1024 with setcap (IPv6 access-safe)

$ setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' /path/to/dnsseed
